# Line-ending policy for the generator repository (added 2026-10-19, cpp-pinyin finding F32).
#
# WHY: the eight generated tables are a CROSS-REPOSITORY byte contract -- this repository produces
# them, `tools/check_drift.ps1` compares them with cpp-pinyin's `data/dialects/**` byte for byte.
# Measured before this file existed: every tracked text file here was `i/lf` in the index but `w/crlf`
# in a Windows working copy, and the generators wrote CRLF in text mode. Both sides were wrong in
# the same direction, so the comparison passed while the bytes were not the ones cpp-pinyin ships.
#
# `text eol=lf` on sources (they are read by tools, diffed, and run under bash on the board) and on
# the input tables under data/ (they are parsed line by line; a CRLF read is a stray `\r` on every
# key). The OUTPUT tables also match `*.txt` below, but that rule is NOT what makes them LF:
# they live in out/ (ignored, so git never touches them), and `write_lf_atomically()` is what
# guarantees their line endings. Measured with `git check-attr`: the glob DOES resolve for
# out/dict/mandarin/word.txt -- which is exactly why the guarantee must not rest on it.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
